Steve Titcombe asks how his death on level 1 from a poisen dart trap
was his fault...   

It wasn't; the dungeon is an inherently dangerous place.  But it
illustrates a point about what to wish for.   Wise wishing is
dependent on where you are in the game.

Early in the game, playing as a Wizard, I'll usually wish for either
of 2 things:
a)  blessed +3 green dragon scale mail
b)  blessed +3 helm of opposite alignment

(a) is because my single most common cause of early death is poison,
with starvation, (or risky things done from lack of food, or using up
my prayer turns on hunger) being high up there.  The green-mail gives
intrinsic poison resistance.  Those damn Uruk-hai become much less of
a worry, and I can now safely eat kobold corpses, acid blob corpses,
etc for food.  (but yellow mold corpses still are off my diet...)

The other intrinsics may be harder to get, but I'm much less likely to
die from them. (and wizards already have magic resistance).
								
(b) is because lawful alters are hard to find.  But a helm of opposite
alignment allows one to convert alters to lawful, and from thence to
bless all my scrolls, potions, weapons, etc.
   And this helm allows other sneaky things.  Take it off, put it on a
couple times and you're chaotic - go kill a couple peaceful creatures,
rob a store.  Even kill a white unicorn to whom you've fed gems.  Then
take it off, put it on, you're lawful again, and the gods are still
chummy - cuz you've never done anything against your alignment.

-----

Steve, if you'd only wished for green dragon scale mail, you'd be a
demi-god today.

